'\" t
.TH "ACTON\-FULL\-LOCALNET" "1"
.nh
.ad l
.ss \n[.ss] 0
.SH "NAME"
acton\-full\-localnet \[em] Run and manage a local TON network with real validators
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
\fBacton full\-localnet\fR [\fIoptions\fR] \fIcommand\fR
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
Full localnet runs TON validators, consensus, elections, and a TON Center
v2/v3 API stack in Docker. Use it to develop contracts and applications that
depend on full\-node behavior or indexed chain data.
.sp
Each named network has its own blockchain, ports, nodes, and snapshots.
Acton manages the Docker containers and a local control service. The CLI
works independently of Acton Studio.
.sp
The network starts with a genesis validator. You can add full nodes and
validators while it runs. Local funds are measured in GRAM.
.sp
Use \fBacton simulated\-localnet\fR for fast local execution, public\-network
forks, or manual control of blocks and time. Full localnet uses real TON
block production and requires more CPU, memory, disk space, and startup time.
.SH "QUICK START"
Install Docker Desktop or Docker Engine with Compose v2. Start the engine
and check that these commands succeed:
.sp
.RS 4
.nf
docker info
docker compose version
.fi
.RE
.sp
Start a network in the current project:
.sp
.RS 4
.nf
acton full\-localnet start dev
.fi
.RE
.sp
Acton creates the network, downloads missing images, starts the containers,
and waits for the node and APIs to become ready. The first start can take
several minutes. The command prints the network ID and endpoint URLs.
.sp
Keep this terminal open while you work. Press Ctrl\-C to stop the network
gracefully. To run it in the background instead, use:
.sp
.RS 4
.nf
acton full\-localnet start dev \-\-detach
acton full\-localnet status dev
acton full\-localnet stop dev
.fi
.RE
.sp
Run \fBacton full\-localnet start dev\fR again to resume the saved blockchain.
.SH "SELECTING A NETWORK"
Most subcommands accept a network name or ID. Names are scoped to the state
directory. Use \fBacton full\-localnet list\fR to find saved networks.
.sp
When you omit the network, Acton selects the only saved network. If several
networks exist, an interactive terminal shows a selector. Non\-interactive
commands and \fB\-\-json\fR require an explicit name or ID when selection is ambiguous.
.sp
If no networks exist, \fBstart\fR without a name creates one named \fBlocalnet\fR\&.
An unused name passed to \fBstart\fR creates a separate network.
.sp
For node and snapshot commands, put the network before the nested action:
.sp
.RS 4
.nf
acton full\-localnet node dev add worker
acton full\-localnet snapshot dev list
.fi
.RE
.sp
The \fBoperation\fR command selects its network with \fB\-\-network\fR:
.sp
.RS 4
.nf
acton full\-localnet operation <OPERATION_ID> \-\-network dev
.fi
.RE
.SH "OPTIONS"
.sp
\fB\-\-state\-dir\fR \fIpath\fR
.RS 4
Directory containing network definitions, control\-service records, and logs.
The default is \fB\&.acton\-localnet\fR inside the resolved project root.
.sp
Use the same directory for subsequent commands that manage these networks.
Blockchain databases and snapshot archives are stored in Docker volumes.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-json\fR
.RS 4
Print machine\-readable results and disable interactive progress and selection.
This option does not make operations asynchronous. Use \fBstart \-\-detach\fR when
a script must continue after startup.
.sp
Network records include \fBid\fR, \fBname\fR, \fBstatus\fR, \fBendpoints\fR, \fBnodes\fR,
\fBoperation\fR, \fBsnapshotOperation\fR, and \fBerror\fR\&. Inspect \fBstatus\fR to determine
whether a saved network is running.
.RE
.SH "SUBCOMMANDS"
.SS "acton full-localnet start"
Create or resume a network and wait for its APIs.
.SS "Synopsis"
\fBacton full\-localnet start\fR [\fIname\fR] [\fIoptions\fR]
.sp
If this command launches the control service, it stays in the foreground
until shutdown. Ctrl\-C stops the service and network gracefully. If a service
already manages the network, \fBstart\fR returns when the network is ready.
.sp
Creation options apply only to a new network. To restart an existing network,
pass its name without those options. To change its initial accounts, ports,
or genesis timing, create a network with a different name.
.SS "Options"
.sp
\fB\-\-detach\fR
.RS 4
Leave a newly started control service and network running in the background
after readiness checks finish. Use \fBstop\fR to shut them down later.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-port\-base\fR \fIport\fR
.RS 4
First of five consecutive host ports for Config, Admin, API v2, API v3,
and the observability dashboard, in that order.
.sp
Acton automatically selects an available range starting at 19000 when omitted.
Saved networks reserve their assigned ports even while stopped.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-block\-time\-ms\fR \fIms\fR
.RS 4
Target interval between blocks, in milliseconds. The value must be greater
than zero. The bundled Localton image defaults to 1000 ms.
.sp
Actual block timing depends on consensus and available resources. This option
sets the target in the initial network configuration.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-election\-time\-seconds\fR \fIseconds\fR
.RS 4
Validator round duration, in seconds. The minimum is 4 seconds. The bundled
Localton image defaults to 120 seconds.
.sp
This value also sets the initial election windows and stake freeze period.
With 120 seconds, elections open 90 seconds before the validator set changes
and close 30 seconds before the change.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-accounts\fR \fIname\fR[,\fIname\fR\[u2026]]_
.RS 4
Project wallets to initialize and fund with 100 GRAM each. Defaults to
\fB[localnet].accounts\fR in \fBActon.toml\fR\&. An explicit list replaces the configuration
list. See \fBStartup Accounts\fR for wallet setup and persistence.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-accounts\-file\fR \fIpath\fR
.RS 4
JSON file containing an array of hexadecimal \fBShardAccount\fR BoCs to import
into the initial blockchain state. See \fBAccount Imports\fR for the format.
.RE
.SS "acton full-localnet create"
Save a stopped network definition without starting Docker containers.
Use this when you want to reserve the name, ports, and genesis inputs before
the first start.
.SS "Synopsis"
\fBacton full\-localnet create\fR [\fIname\fR] [\fIoptions\fR]
.sp
Accepts the creation options documented under \fBstart\fR: \fB\-\-port\-base\fR,
\fB\-\-block\-time\-ms\fR, \fB\-\-election\-time\-seconds\fR, \fB\-\-accounts\fR, and \fB\-\-accounts\-file\fR\&.
The default name is \fBlocalnet\fR\&. Wallets and import files are read when the
definition is created.
.sp
.RS 4
.nf
acton full\-localnet create staging \-\-port\-base 19100
acton full\-localnet start staging \-\-detach
.fi
.RE
.SS "acton full-localnet list"
List saved network definitions as a JSON array. This command works while
Docker is stopped and does not start any services.
.SS "Synopsis"
\fBacton full\-localnet list\fR
.sp
Use \fBstatus\fR to check a network\[cq]s current runtime state.
.SS "acton full-localnet status"
Show network status, endpoint URLs, and state location. With \fB\-\-json\fR, the
record also includes nodes, operations, configuration, and diagnostic details.
.SS "Synopsis"
\fBacton full\-localnet status\fR [\fInetwork\fR]
.sp
This command can inspect a network while its control service is stopped.
It does not start the network. Endpoint URLs remain in the record while
the corresponding services are stopped.
.SS "acton full-localnet stop"
Stop the selected network and its control service gracefully. Blockchain
data and snapshots remain available for the next start.
.SS "Synopsis"
\fBacton full\-localnet stop\fR [\fInetwork\fR]
.SS "acton full-localnet shutdown"
Shut down the selected network and its control service. Like \fBstop\fR, this
command preserves persistent data and waits for shutdown to finish.
.SS "Synopsis"
\fBacton full\-localnet shutdown\fR [\fInetwork\fR]
.SS "acton full-localnet delete"
Delete the network\[cq]s containers, blockchain volumes, and snapshot archives.
This removes the network from the list of available networks.
.SS "Synopsis"
\fBacton full\-localnet delete\fR [\fInetwork\fR] [\fB\-\-yes\fR]
.sp
The command asks for confirmation in an interactive terminal. Pass \fB\-\-yes\fR
to confirm deletion in a script. Use \fBstop\fR when you intend to resume the
network later.
.SS "acton full-localnet logs"
Show the last lines of the saved network operation log. This command reads
the log once and exits. It also works when the control service is stopped.
.SS "Synopsis"
\fBacton full\-localnet logs\fR [\fInetwork\fR] [\fB\-\-tail\fR \fIlines\fR]
.sp
\fB\-\-tail\fR defaults to 100 lines. With \fB\-\-json\fR, the result is an object with
a \fBlogs\fR string.
.sp
.RS 4
.nf
acton full\-localnet logs dev \-\-tail 200
.fi
.RE
.SS "acton full-localnet node"
Manage additional full nodes and validator participation. Start the network
before managing its nodes. Use \fBstatus \-\-json\fR to find node IDs. The genesis
validator belongs to the network and cannot be removed with \fBnode remove\fR\&.
.SS "Synopsis"
\fBacton full\-localnet node\fR [\fInetwork\fR] \fIcommand\fR
.SS "Subcommands"
.sp
.RS 4
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\fBadd <name> [\-\-validator]\fR joins a new full node and prints its ID. With
\fB\-\-validator\fR, the node also participates in validator elections.
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\fBenter\-validation <id>\fR enables election participation for an existing node.
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\fBleave\-validation <id>\fR disables future election participation. The node
continues its duties until it leaves the elected validator sets.
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\fBremove <id> \-\-yes [\-\-force]\fR deletes an additional node and its state.
Normally, a validator must first leave validation and exit both the current
and next elected sets. \fB\-\-force\fR bypasses this check and can disrupt consensus.
.RE
.sp
Election changes take effect through TON election rounds. Completion of
\fBenter\-validation\fR does not mean the node has already become an elected validator.
Use the observability dashboard to inspect election membership.
.sp
.RS 4
.nf
acton full\-localnet node dev add validator\-b \-\-validator
acton full\-localnet status dev \-\-json
acton full\-localnet node dev leave\-validation <NODE_ID>
.fi
.RE
.sp
After the node leaves both elected sets, remove it:
.sp
.RS 4
.nf
acton full\-localnet node dev remove <NODE_ID> \-\-yes
.fi
.RE
.SS "acton full-localnet snapshot"
Save and restore the blockchain state and node topology. Full localnet
snapshots are compressed archives of node databases stored in Docker volumes.
.SS "Synopsis"
\fBacton full\-localnet snapshot\fR [\fInetwork\fR] \fIcommand\fR
.SS "Subcommands"
.sp
.RS 4
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\fBlist\fR prints saved snapshots with their IDs, names, creation times, block
information, and sizes.
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\fBcreate [name]\fR saves a snapshot. If the network is running, Acton stops
its nodes, creates the archives, then starts it again. If already stopped,
it remains stopped. The optional name labels the saved state.
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\fBrestore <id> \-\-yes\fR replaces the current blockchain state and node topology
with the saved state. Acton stops the network, restores the archives,
resets the indexer, and waits for startup and API readiness.
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\fBdelete <id> \-\-yes\fR removes one saved snapshot\[cq]s archives and metadata.
.RE
.sp
Use the snapshot ID returned by \fBcreate\fR or \fBlist\fR for restore and delete.
The display name is a label, not an identifier.
.sp
Snapshot commands also work when the control service is stopped. Acton
starts a temporary service and closes it when the command finishes.
After a restore through this temporary service, the network is stopped again.
Run \fBstart\fR to continue working with it.
.sp
Creation and restoration can take several minutes. Allow disk space for
the current databases, compressed archives, and temporary restore backups.
Restoration rebuilds indexed data from the saved blockchain.
.sp
.RS 4
.nf
acton full\-localnet snapshot dev create before\-upgrade
acton full\-localnet snapshot dev list
acton full\-localnet snapshot dev restore <SNAPSHOT_ID> \-\-yes
.fi
.RE
.SS "acton full-localnet operation"
Inspect a saved operation or wait for it to finish. Operations record
progress phases, timing, results, and errors for network changes.
.SS "Synopsis"
\fBacton full\-localnet operation\fR \fIid\fR [\fB\-\-network\fR \fInetwork\fR] [\fB\-\-wait\fR]
.sp
Without \fB\-\-wait\fR, this command prints the saved operation record as JSON.
With \fB\-\-wait\fR, it waits for a running operation and exits with an error if
the operation fails. Waiting requires the operation\[cq]s control service to be running.
.sp
Find operation IDs in JSON command results or the network\[cq]s JSON status.
Startup, node changes, and snapshot commands already wait for their operations.
.sp
.RS 4
.nf
acton full\-localnet operation <OPERATION_ID> \-\-network dev \-\-wait
.fi
.RE
.SH "STARTUP ACCOUNTS"
Create a development wallet, then select it when you create the network:
.sp
.RS 4
.nf
acton wallet new \-\-name deployer \-\-local
acton full\-localnet start dev \-\-accounts deployer \-\-detach
.fi
.RE
.sp
For several wallets, use a comma\-separated list:
.sp
.RS 4
.nf
acton full\-localnet start wallets \-\-accounts deployer,alice,bob \-\-detach
.fi
.RE
.sp
You can set the default CLI selection in \fBActon.toml\fR:
.sp
.RS 4
.nf
[localnet]
accounts = ["deployer", "alice", "bob"]
.fi
.RE
.sp
Wallets must exist in Acton\[cq]s wallet configuration. Acton resolves their
initial state and gives each selected wallet 100 GRAM in the zerostate.
This is the blockchain\[cq]s initial state, before block production begins.
.sp
Restarts use the saved state. They do not replenish spent balances or reread
the account selection. Use a snapshot to restore a prepared test balance,
or create a new network for a different initial selection.
.sp
The \fB[localnet].accounts\fR default applies to CLI creation. Studio uses the
wallet selection submitted in its creation form. Full localnet timing and
ports come from the creation flags above, rather than other \fB[localnet]\fR settings.
.SH "ACCOUNT IMPORTS"
Use \fB\-\-accounts\-file\fR to include existing contract states in the zerostate.
The file must contain a JSON array of strings. Each string is a complete
hexadecimal \fBShardAccount\fR BoC, including the account\[cq]s code, data, and balance.
.sp
An address, a base64 string, or a contract data cell alone is not a
\fBShardAccount\fR import. Supply active basechain accounts with distinct addresses.
Imported accounts and startup wallets share the same initial address space.
.sp
.RS 4
.nf
acton full\-localnet start imported \-\-accounts\-file accounts.json \-\-detach
.fi
.RE
.sp
Imports copy account state into a new local blockchain. They do not load
public\-network transaction history or fetch later changes from that network.
Imported balances come from the supplied account state.
.SH "CONNECTING SCRIPTS AND APPLICATIONS"
Read the endpoint URLs from \fBstart\fR or \fBstatus\fR\&. With \fB\-\-port\-base 19000\fR,
Acton assigns these endpoints:
.sp
.RS 4
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'Config: \fBhttp://127.0.0.1:19000\fR for network configuration and the faucet.
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'Admin: \fBhttp://127.0.0.1:19001\fR for Localton administration.
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'API v2: \fBhttp://127.0.0.1:19002/api/v2\fR for TON Center v2 queries and message
submission.
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'API v3: \fBhttp://127.0.0.1:19003/api/v3\fR for indexed transactions and account data.
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'Dashboard: \fBhttp://127.0.0.1:19004\fR for node health, synchronization, and elections.
.RE
.sp
The ports bind to loopback. Use a local Docker engine so Acton can reach
these endpoints. Port assignments belong to the saved network, so use its
reported URLs when several networks exist.
.sp
To target this network with Acton scripts, add its API URLs to \fBActon.toml\fR:
.sp
.RS 4
.nf
[networks.localnet]
api.v2 = "http://127.0.0.1:19002/api/v2"
api.v3 = "http://127.0.0.1:19003/api/v3"
.fi
.RE
.sp
Then run your deployment script with the local network selected:
.sp
.RS 4
.nf
acton script \-\-net localnet scripts/deploy.tolk
.fi
.RE
.sp
For an additional named connection, use \fB[networks.dev]\fR with the same fields
and select it with \fB\-\-net custom:dev\fR\&. The network global ID is \fB\-3\fR, which
is also Acton\[cq]s default for custom networks.
.sp
You can check the APIs directly:
.sp
.RS 4
.nf
curl \-\-fail http://127.0.0.1:19002/api/v2/getMasterchainInfo
curl \-\-fail http://127.0.0.1:19003/api/v3/masterchainInfo
.fi
.RE
.SH "PERSISTENCE AND DOCKER"
Acton stores network definitions and logs under \fB\&.acton\-localnet/networks/\fR
in the project. \fB\-\-state\-dir\fR selects another catalog. Each network directory
contains its configuration, service records, operation records, and startup logs.
.sp
Docker volumes hold the live blockchain databases, indexer data, and snapshot
archives. Copying \fB\&.acton\-localnet\fR alone does not back up the network.
Use the snapshot commands for local restore points.
.sp
The Docker target is selected from \fBDOCKER_CONTEXT\fR, then \fBDOCKER_HOST\fR, then
the active Docker context. Acton saves the target and image when it prepares
the deployment. Restarts reuse that identity to find the correct containers
and volumes. Changing the active context does not move an existing network.
.sp
\fBACTON_LOCALNET_IMAGE\fR selects a custom Localton image for a new deployment.
It must provide the Localton commands and APIs expected by this Acton version.
The default is the versioned Localton image bundled with Acton.
.SH "AUTOMATION"
Use an explicit name, a separate state directory, \fB\-\-json\fR, and \fB\-\-detach\fR
for automated jobs. Wait for \fBstart\fR to succeed before sending API requests.
.sp
.RS 4
.nf
acton full\-localnet \-\-state\-dir /tmp/acton\-ci \-\-json \(rs
  start ci \-\-port\-base 19200 \-\-detach
acton full\-localnet \-\-state\-dir /tmp/acton\-ci \-\-json status ci
acton full\-localnet \-\-state\-dir /tmp/acton\-ci stop ci
.fi
.RE
.sp
For a fresh blockchain per job, choose a unique network name and delete
that network during job cleanup:
.sp
.RS 4
.nf
acton full\-localnet \-\-state\-dir /tmp/acton\-ci delete ci \-\-yes
.fi
.RE
.sp
Successful \fBstart\fR output is a network record. Mutating commands return an
operation record in JSON mode. Progress and diagnostics go to stderr.
Check the exit status before using a result as a successful operation.
.SH "TROUBLESHOOTING"
.SS "Docker or image startup fails"
Check \fBdocker info\fR, \fBdocker compose version\fR, and \fBdocker context ls\fR\&.
Verify that the selected engine is running and accessible to your user.
For an image download failure, check the registry and connection details in
the reported error. The first start needs access to download missing images.
.sp
After fixing Docker or the connection, retry \fBstart\fR with the existing name.
Keep its creation options out of the restart command.
.SS "A port range is unavailable"
Another process or saved network already uses the requested range. Choose
five free consecutive ports with \fB\-\-port\-base\fR when creating a new network.
For an existing network, stop the conflicting process before restarting it.
.SS "Genesis options are rejected"
The name identifies an existing network. Run \fBstart <name>\fR to resume it,
or use a new name to apply different initial accounts or timing.
.SS "Startup or restore is taking too long"
Inspect status and the operation log:
.sp
.RS 4
.nf
acton full\-localnet status dev \-\-json
acton full\-localnet logs dev \-\-tail 200
.fi
.RE
.sp
Check available Docker disk space and memory. Startup includes readiness
checks for the TON node and APIs. Restore also rebuilds the indexer.
Use the dashboard URL from \fBstatus\fR to inspect synchronization progress.
.SS "The control service stopped unexpectedly"
Use \fBstatus\fR and \fBlogs\fR to inspect the saved network. Startup errors include
the full log path. The network directory also contains \fBservice.log\fR and
launcher logs for failures before node startup.
.sp
Retry \fBstart <name>\fR after resolving the reported problem. If snapshot
recovery fails, preserve the reported files and logs for diagnosis. Acton
blocks startup while an incomplete restore could leave inconsistent node data.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.sp
.RS 4
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\fB0\fR: The command completed successfully. For \fBstatus\fR, a stopped network
is a valid result. Inspect the JSON \fBstatus\fR field to check readiness.
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'Nonzero: Argument parsing, network selection, startup, or an operation failed.
\fBoperation \-\-wait\fR also fails when the saved operation has failed.
.RE
.SH "DISPLAY OPTIONS"
.sp
\fB\-\-color\fR \fIwhen\fR
.RS 4
Control when to use colored output.
.sp
Possible values: \fBauto\fR, \fBalways\fR, \fBnever\fR
.RE
.SH "PROJECT OPTIONS"
.sp
\fB\-\-manifest\-path\fR \fIpath\fR
.RS 4
Path to the \fBActon.toml\fR file to use for this invocation.
.sp
Use this when running the command outside the project directory or when the
manifest lives at a non\-default location.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-project\-root\fR \fIpath\fR
.RS 4
Path to the project root to use for configuration discovery and relative
defaults.
.sp
This conflicts with \fB\-\-manifest\-path\fR\&.
.RE
.SH "SEE ALSO"
acton(1), acton\-simulated\-localnet(1), acton\-wallet(1), acton\-script(1), acton\-rpc(1)
